when:
A change does either of these, and does not add or update the docs that describe it:
- adds a user-visible capability, such as a new model type or modality, a new mode, or an integration that users must enable, configure or use differently;
- changes behavior that users or operators observe, or that existing docs describe, such as a protocol, failure detection or recovery, configuration semantics, or a persisted or on-disk format.

expect:
Ask the author to do the following in the same PR:
- update every existing doc that describes the changed behavior (design docs, overview pages, the configuration reference, protocol READMEs), not only the one next to the changed code;
- for a new capability without docs, add a user-facing page or section covering what is supported, the configuration or flags that enable it, and a minimal usage example;
- document the consequences users will hit:
  - timing bounds, stated in terms of the setting that controls them;
  - which state is lost or must be rebuilt after recovery;
  - manual or migration steps an incompatible change requires, such as clearing data written by an older version.

Require a docstring on every public method that states its contract:
- what the method does;
- a description of each parameter;
- the return value, or that the method returns nothing;
- errors raised, where relevant.
A one-line summary that leaves parameters and return value undocumented is not enough. For interfaces, Protocols and abstract base classes, also require precise parameter and return types instead of catch-all types such as object or Any, using concrete classes, Protocols, TypeVars or unions as needed.

Ask the author to:
- bump the format or metadata version written with each entry;
- on read, detect old-format entries, or entries missing a field that the current configuration requires;
- reject or skip those entries (treat them as a miss) instead of silently filling a default such as None and continuing.
Flag code that defaults a missing required field for backward compatibility. Accept silent defaulting only for a field that is optional for every consumer that reads it.

Ask the author to:
- identify which thread runs the new code;
- verify, from the object's implementation rather than by assumption, that the object is safe to use from that thread, and state it.
If the object is not safe, or wraps a non-thread-safe resource, require one of:
- a lock around every use of the object;
- a separate per-thread instance;
- handing the work back to the owning thread, e.g. through a queue or dispatcher.

Ask the author to:
