- remove the polling loop, together with the interval, timeout and warning parameters that exist only to drive it.

when:
A change generalizes or restructures code beyond what its current real implementations need. It does any of the following:
- adds abstractions, interfaces, planners or new intermediate state that other modules access directly;
- generalizes logic that has only one real implementation, is specific to one device or vendor, or is experimental and still subject to change;
- rewrites existing working functionality into a more general or more complicated form so it can be shared with a new path.

expect:
Ask the author to:
- drop the abstraction and keep single-implementation, vendor-specific or experimental logic concrete until it stabilizes and further real implementations exist;
- reuse existing working functions as they are, by importing them, moving them unchanged into a shared utils module, or duplicating them, and leave the shared abstraction for a later refactor;
- remove new intermediate state that other modules read directly when it only serves the abstraction or existing components already provide that information.
Flag rewrites whose result is more complicated than the code they replace.

when:
A change adds or moves functions, methods or classes in a source file or class, new or existing, so that they end up out of the layout below. Typical cases:
- a new private helper method inserted between public methods of a class;
- a public entry point placed among private helpers;
- private helpers interleaved with public classes and entry-point functions;
- a new or substantially reorganized file whose definitions are in arbitrary order.

expect:
Require this layout:
1. imports;
2. private module-level helper functions and classes (prefixed `_`);
3. public classes, each ordered as `__init__`, then public methods, then private `_` methods at the end of the class;
4. public module-level functions used by other modules.

Ask the author to move each added or moved definition to its place in this layout; for example, new private helper methods go to the last part of the class. This may be raised as a nit. Do not ask to reorder existing code the diff does not touch.

when:
A change adds a fallback, degraded or alternative code path that runs when something the code expects is missing or fails, and continues instead of failing. This applies in either case:
- a dependency, compiled extension or capability is missing, although the surrounding feature cannot work without it, so it is present in every configuration where this code can run;
- a resource or setting the user explicitly requested cannot be provided or is unsupported, and the code silently continues with something different from what was requested. Examples of such settings are a memory or allocation option, a size, a placement, or a mode.

expect:
Ask the author why the trivial case gets a different representation. Ask them to:
- always build the general structure, including for the trivial case (e.g. a map or list with one entry);
- drop the None or sentinel;
- remove the downstream checks and duplicated branches that exist only to handle it, so every case goes through one code path.
